Greater Trochanteric Pain Syndrome (GTPS)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Lateral hip pain, Point tenderness over greater trochanter, Pain exacerbation lying on affected side

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-026
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute-on-chronic exacerbation of lateral hip pain causing total sleep deprivation and sudden inability to ambulate independently.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage established that the patient suffered a severe flare of lateral hip pain rendering them unable to walk or lie in bed, leading to acute distress and exhaustion. Urgent physical assessment was necessary to rule out an occult femoral neck fracture or acute septic bursitis, and to provide immediate, potent analgesia and mechanical offloading to restore basic functional safety prior to regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hip has been aching for months, but tonight the pain is so sharp and burning I can't walk, and if I try to lie down, it feels like a knife is driving into the bone."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute exacerbation of chronic right lateral hip pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of dull, intermittent aching over the lateral thigh present for 4 months; steadily worsened over the past week, causing significant sleep disturbance. Tonight, the pain abruptly escalated into a severe, constant, sharp burning sensation. Exacerbation of pain is profound when attempting to lie on the affected right side, making sleep impossible. Patient reports extreme pain aggravation during weight-bearing, specifically upon rising from a seated position, and describes pain radiating down the lateral thigh, stopping above the knee.
Functional Status: Acutely immobilized in a recliner chair; unable to bear weight without extreme pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to severe antalgic gait and sudden pain-induced limb yielding.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, visibly exhausted, sitting asymmetrically to offload the right hip.
Physical Exam: Right hip reveals focal, exquisite point tenderness directly palpated over the greater trochanter of the femur. Reproduction of severe pain with resisted active hip abduction and external rotation. Passive internal/external rotation of the hip joint is relatively preserved and painless, confirming extra-articular pathology. Positive single-leg stance test: patient is unable to stand unsupported on the right limb for even 5 seconds due to immediate, severe lateral hip pain and yielding. Unilateral antalgic gait with a pronounced Trendelenburg-like lurch observed when ambulating with support. No overlying erythema, warmth, or palpable fluctuant bursal swelling.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s bed mattress is notably firm, contributing to the lateral compression pain.
Medication Reconciliation: Regular paracetamol and oral NSAIDs (Ibuprofen) utilized over the past 48 hours with minimal efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Greater Trochanteric Pain Syndrome (GTPS) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Occult Femoral Neck Fracture - Ruled out by the absence of trauma, preserved (though guarded) active straight leg raise, and pain localized strictly to the lateral trochanter rather than deep groin pain on axial loading.

Hip Joint Osteoarthritis - Ruled out clinically by the preservation of passive internal rotation without deep groin pain or hard bony end-feel restrictions.

Lumbar Radiculopathy (L4/L5) - Ruled out by normal lower limb reflexes, intact dermatomal sensation, negative straight leg raise, and pain terminating above the knee.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing pain crisis localized to the peritrochanteric space, characteristic of an acute gluteal tendinopathy/bursitis flare (GTPS). The critical clinical step was differentiating this severe lateral pain from an acute intra-articular pathology or occult fracture. Physical examination clearly isolated the pain to the abductor mechanism (resisted abduction, point tenderness over the trochanter) with preserved passive joint mechanics. The severity of the pain resulted in total sleep deprivation and an inability to safely ambulate, creating an acute functional crisis. Postponing evaluation would have left the patient stranded, exhausted, and at high risk of a fall. Urgent after-hours assessment provided diagnostic validation, ruled out surgical emergencies, and initiated aggressive, targeted pharmacological and mechanical management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT to rapidly decrease acute peritrochanteric inflammation. Prescribed a short course of Codeine 30mg PRN strictly for nocturnal pain to facilitate sleep.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Sleep with a thick, firm pillow securely placed between the knees and ankles when lying on the unaffected side. Consequence: Prevention of the painful hip dropping into adduction, keeping the gluteal tendons slack and immediately stopping the sharp tension pain that causes waking (negative reinforcement).
Action: Use a walking stick in the hand opposite to the painful hip. Consequence: Mechanical offloading of the inflamed abductor tendons, resulting in a significantly smoother, less painful walking pattern (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral recommended for long-term gluteal load management and strengthening.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided a properly measured walking stick.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7-10 days for consideration of an ultrasound-guided corticosteroid injection if severe nocturnal symptoms persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient is entirely unable to lift the leg off the bed, if deep groin pain develops at rest, or if systemic fevers occur.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, reflecting tendon overload and potential secondary bursitis. An ultrasound is required to definitively assess the extent of tendon wear. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, burning pain will subside with the injection and altered sleeping mechanics over 3-5 days. Long-term resolution requires specific, guided exercises to strengthen the gluteal tendons to handle daily loads.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you suddenly cannot lift your leg at all, if the pain shifts deep into your groin, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tendons attaching to the side of your hip bone have become severely inflamed and irritated from compression. The injected medication rapidly suppresses this chemical fire. The pillow between your knees is essential because it stops the leg from crossing the midline, taking the physical stretch off those inflamed tendons while you sleep.
Actionable Steps: Do not sleep on the painful side. You must use the thick pillow between your knees every night. Use the walking stick in your "good" hand to take the bodyweight off the damaged tendons when walking.

